WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.677
The inner query returns patient IDs from Cardiology admissions.

00:00:03.677 --> 00:00:07.542
The outer query keeps patients whose patient ID appears in that set.

00:00:07.542 --> 00:00:11.537
This is a natural use of IN because the business question is membership.

00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:16.102
Run the query and connect each returned patient with the Cardiology admissions table.

00:00:16.102 --> 00:00:20.387
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:25.552
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:25.552 --> 00:00:27.632
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:27.632 --> 00:00:33.132
In a real setting such as a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up,

00:00:33.132 --> 00:00:38.657
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:38.657 --> 00:00:40.577
before discussing syntax.

00:00:40.577 --> 00:00:46.080
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
